  "explanation": "W=1.6 GeV, Q2=1.0 GeV2, cos(theta*)=0.5, phi*=90 deg is a physically valid eta-electroproduction kinematic point: W is above the ep->e'p(eta) threshold (938+548=1486 MeV) and well within the EtaMAID-2023 table grid (W=1.4856-6 GeV, Q2=0-5 GeV2) referenced in eta/mpintp.inc, and it lies within the accessible kinematic range for a 6.53 GeV CLAS12 beam at this Q2 (E'_min ~ Q2/4E gives W_max ~ 3.5 GeV). I generated a single-point EXCLURAD input file (input_eta_W1.6_Q2_1.0_cos0.5_phi90.dat) using the eta-channel 'standard CLAS12' settings documented in eta/README.md and eta/examples/README.md: model=3 (maid2000/EtaMAID), rc_mode=0 (full O(alpha) RC), bmom=6.53 GeV, tmom=0.0 (fixed target), lepton=1 (electron), ivec=1 (detect recoil proton), vcut=0.166 GeV^2 (CLAS12 eta-analysis inelasticity cut). No source or format corrections were needed; the file follows the documented 7-parameter header + N-point kinematic block + trailing NAG-artifact terminator line required by the Fortran reader. Flag: the eta/ directory in this environment does not contain the maid07-PPpi.tbl hadronic-model table that exclurad.F's maid_lee routine reads at runtime for model=3/ivec=1 (per eta/README.md this file should hold the EtaMAID-2023 multipole tables); the input file itself is correct and ready to run, but actually executing it against the compiled exclurad.exe will fail until that table file is supplied alongside the source. No build/run was attempted since no compiler or shell is available in this environment."
}

